{
  "id": "ss.lib.spider-arm-widow",
  "name": "Widow's arm",
  "description": "The spider hand's arm after Widow's Grip: the same skeleton as ss.lib.spider-arm, hung from the same socket and reaching the same way, but an older animal wearing it. Chitin banding rings the femur where one segment of shell overlaps the next, a spur stands off the back of the knee, the bristles come in five rather than three, and the foot closes on three toes instead of two. The colour is the one register no other weapon in the roster has taken — a bruised carapace purple carrying dark red, with the talons in blood — which is what a hand that drinks what it bites should look like. A separate document rather than a variant: the marks are geometry, not fills, and a variant that has to add parts to say what it is has stopped being a variant. The bones are swept along opposite bows so a shallow S runs through the limb the way it does on a leg that arches out of the body and settles back onto its foot; they taper along that curve rather than stepping down at the joint; the knee is a narrow hinge rather than a bearing; and the foot is a hook with a shorter barb opposing it, because what closes on a body here is a grab and a grab has a long side and a short one. Bones darken outward the way the Scolopend's fringe does, which is depth rather than light: a limb the parent turns to any angle cannot carry a lit edge without the light turning with it.",
  "tags": ["ss-lib"],
  "size": [32, 20],
  "anchor": [0.125, 0.7],
